Date: 12 November 2026
Project: Frontier Model Accord (FMA)
Record type: Dated archival closure record
Status: Closed and locked as a frozen reference record
Authority: Project-level closure record; not treaty text and not an amendment to Annex III
1. Purpose and scope

This record documents the formal closure of Phase II — Cross-Provision Harmonization. Phase II addressed system-level consistency across four harmonization modules:

    Module A — Institutional Authority and Emergency Delegation
    Module B — Independent Technical and Actuarial Governance
    Module C — Ledger and Accounting Interoperability
    Module D — Domestic Implementation and Cross-Border Enforceability

All four modules are closed. The corrected Final System-Level Governance Synthesis is accepted and locked as the definitive Phase II closing audit record.

This phase concerns documentary and governance harmonization. It must not be confused with the separately identified Phase 2 independent validation roadmap, comprising VAL-001 through VAL-010 in the Public Accountability Edition.
2. Permanent audit register

The permanent audit register entries AUD-SMEF-219 through AUD-SMEF-295 remain preserved with their individual recorded dispositions and qualifications.

Closure of the four harmonization modules does not mean that every underlying audit item is substantively resolved. Existing OPEN and PARTIAL statuses remain intact. No status has been upgraded merely because Phase II has closed.

This public closure record summarizes the register's preservation and does not reproduce or replace the controlling register.
3. Final precision corrections incorporated into the closing synthesis

The Final System-Level Governance Synthesis was accepted with the following precision corrections:

    Article 7 ter — treaty obligations and domestic implementation. Internal treaty-level claim and transaction rules remain distinct from domestic implementation, direct domestic effect, perfection, and third-party enforceability. A participating State's international obligation to implement a treaty rule does not, by itself, establish that the rule has direct effect in that State's domestic law.

    Article 7 quater — Chief Actuary authority. The Chief Actuary's proposed binding authority is limited to actuarial measurement, valuation, and reporting standards. It does not confer authority to adjudicate legal ownership, control custodial assets, register property interests, encumber or transfer assets, or displace domestic depository, trust, or property law. Actuarial certification does not establish title or legal enforceability.

    Article 10 bis — Stage 6/7 functional separation. General technical standards, approval of a migration-specific restoration protocol, independent empirical observation, and final Stage 7 certification remain separate functions. A Stage 7 certificate is a technical restoration determination, not a judicial order.

These corrections clarify the accepted closing synthesis. They do not amend controlling Annex III.
4. Five operative working successors preserved separately

The following working successors remain separate from controlling Annex III:

    Article 7 bis — Facility Authority
    Article 7 ter — Claim Priority
    Article 7 quater — Actuarial Completeness
    Article 7 quintus — Continuity Enforcement
    Article 10 bis — Migration Execution

They remain working specifications, not adopted treaty law. Their preservation in the project record does not establish domestic enforceability, operational readiness, or independent validation. No consolidated governance amendment or replacement treaty text was authorized by Phase II closure.
5. Controlling text and implementation boundary

The following boundaries remain controlling:

    Annex III remains unchanged.
    No consolidated governance amendment was authorized.
    The five working successors remain separate and unadopted.
    No operational, financial, technical, or domestic legal implementation readiness is established or claimed.
    No independent validation result is inferred from documentary harmonization or closure.

The separately identified independent validation protocols VAL-001 through VAL-010 remain open unless and until their required independent work is completed and recorded through the applicable control process. Closing Phase II Cross-Provision Harmonization does not close those protocols.
6. Phase boundary

Phase II is closed and frozen. No subsequent phase, amendment cycle, new audit register entry, or implementation claim is authorized by this record.

Any subsequent phase must begin only after separate, explicit authorization. Phase II is to be treated as a fixed reference record for that future work; it is not to be silently reopened or modified.
7. Closing statement

Phase II achieved documentary cross-provision harmonization and a locked system-level closing synthesis. It did not establish that the FMA is an enacted treaty, an operating institution, a legally enforceable regime, or a technically or financially validated system.

Final status: PHASE II CLOSED AND LOCKED — FROZEN REFERENCE RECORD.
